---
// Short answers to what people ask before they install. Native details elements, so it works without scripts.
import { DOCS_PATH, repoFile } from "../../site";
---

<section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <h2 id="faq-title">Questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Is Dayhand really free?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. Every app, the server and the command line are free, with no trial. A server on your computer costs
        nothing, and so does a server on Convex's Free plan; its limits are in the
        <a href={repoFile("docs/deploy.md")}>deployment guide</a>. The one paid option planned is
        <a href="#dayhand-cloud">Dayhand Cloud</a>, a server we run for you for $5 a month; it is not open yet.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Why is it free?</summary>
      <p>
        Dayhand is a personal project with no hosted service yet, so there is nothing to bill for. It is shared under the
        Apache License 2.0, and support is what the issues on GitHub offer: bugs you can reproduce and ideas, with no
        promise of a reply time.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need an account?</summary>
      <p>
        Not with us; there are no Dayhand accounts today. A server on your computer needs no account. To reach it from your
        phone you put Tailscale in front of it, which has its own sign-in, and <code>dayhand server deploy</code> asks
        you to log in to your own Convex account.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Where does my data live?</summary>
      <p>
        On your devices and on the server you chose: your computer or your own Convex account. Each app keeps a copy
        for working offline. Nothing goes to us: there is no server of ours yet, and Dayhand Cloud will be a choice
        you make.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Which agents can use it?</summary>
      <p>
        Any MCP client, with the server address and an <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header; Claude Code is one
        command. The Claude apps connect as a custom connector with OAuth, which you approve on the phone. Agents that
        run shell commands can use <code>dayhand</code>, and a ready Agent Skill teaches them how.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can an agent do something I did not allow?</summary>
      <p>
        Inside Dayhand, a policy per agent key allows, asks for or denies each tool, risky steps wait for your answer,
        and one switch pauses every agent. Dayhand cannot stop what an agent does outside Dayhand.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does it work offline?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. The Android, macOS and Linux apps keep the plan on the device and send changes to the server, in order,
        when it can be reached.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is there an app for iPhone or Windows?</summary>
      <p>
        The iPhone app is in the works and not out yet. On Windows, the <code>dayhand</code> command line works with
        a server on your own Convex account; there is no desktop app.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>When can I download a ready-made app?</summary>
      <p>
        The apps have no published release yet and there is no date. The signed Android build is prepared; until it
        is out, the apps build from the source as shown under <a href="#download">Download</a>, beside the commands
        that install the command line.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I see my tasks in Google Calendar?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes, through a read-only calendar feed that any calendar app can subscribe to. Changes are made in Dayhand.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Why do some names still say helm?</summary>
      <p>
        Dayhand was called Helm until backend 0.9.0 and CLI 0.10.0, and the repository moved from
        <code>maiarowsky/helm</code> to <code>maiarowsky/dayhand</code>; old links redirect. The old <code>hlm</code>
        command still works with a warning, and a few names stored in data stay as they were:
        <a href={repoFile("docs/rename.md")}>what changed</a>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Where is the full documentation?</summary>
      <p>
        In <a href={DOCS_PATH}>the documentation</a>: installing, the command line, MCP, the REST API, webhooks, the
        calendar feed and running your own server.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
